./logs/iter_009_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:3458:./logs/iter_009_solver.stdout.log:15:\kappa(C)=\inf_{\mu\in\Delta([0,1])}\max_j
./logs/iter_009_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:3459:./logs/iter_009_solver.stdout.log:22:F(\delta)=\sum_{i=1}^m
./logs/iter_009_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:3460:./logs/iter_009_solver.stdout.log:23:\frac{(c_i-\delta)_+-(c_{i-1}-\delta)_+}{c_i}.
./logs/iter_009_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:3461:./logs/iter_009_solver.stdout.log:25:There is a unique \(\delta_*\in[0,c_m]\) with \(F(\delta_*)=1\). I claim
./logs/iter_009_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:3462:./logs/iter_009_solver.stdout.log:27:\boxed{\kappa(C)=\delta_* .}
./logs/iter_009_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:3463:./logs/iter_009_solver.stdout.log:32:q_i=\frac{(c_i-\delta_*)_+-(c_{i-1}-\delta_*)_+}{c_i}
./logs/iter_009_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:3464:./logs/iter_009_solver.stdout.log:34:on quotes just below \(c_i\). Since \(F(\delta_*)=1\), this is a probability distribution. Against threshold \(c_j\), its expected local payoff is
./logs/iter_009_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:3465:./logs/iter_009_solver.stdout.log:36:\sum_{i\le j} q_i c_i=(c_j-\delta_*)_+,
./logs/iter_009_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:3466:./logs/iter_009_solver.stdout.log:40:c_j-(c_j-\delta_*)_+=\min(c_j,\delta_*)\le\delta_*.
./logs/iter_009_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:3467:./logs/iter_009_solver.stdout.log:42:Thus \(\kappa(C)\le\delta_*\).
./logs/iter_009_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:3468:./logs/iter_009_solver.stdout.log:44:Dual certificate: let \(k=\min\{i:c_i>\delta_*\}\). On the active suffix \(c_k,\ldots,c_m\), define a threshold law by
./logs/iter_009_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:3469:./logs/iter_009_solver.stdout.log:58:c_k+\delta_*,
./logs/iter_009_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:3470:./logs/iter_009_solver.stdout.log:60:where the last equality follows from \(F(\delta_*)=1\). Hence
./logs/iter_009_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:3471:./logs/iter_009_solver.stdout.log:62:\mathbb EZ-\rho(Z)=\delta_*,
./logs/iter_009_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:3472:./logs/iter_009_solver.stdout.log:64:so the dual gives \(\kappa(C)\ge\delta_*\).
./logs/iter_009_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:3474:./logs/iter_009_solver.stdout.log:71:   \(\kappa(C)\) is the unique root value \(\delta_*\) above.
./logs/iter_009_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:3475:./logs/iter_009_solver.stdout.log:74:   Thresholds \(c_j\le\delta_*\) are nonbinding. The dual hard law is a truncated equal-revenue law on the suffix above \(\delta_*\).
./logs/iter_009_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:3476:./logs/iter_009_solver.stdout.log:77:   For \(c_i=r^{m-i}\), \(r=(m-1)/m\), one gets \(\delta_*=r^m=((m-1)/m)^m\), and the primal distribution is uniform over quotes just below the \(c_i\)’s.
./logs/iter_009_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:3477:./logs/iter_009_solver.stdout.log:82:   \max_j R_{M+N+j}\ge N\kappa(C)-M
./logs/iter_009_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:3478:./logs/iter_009_solver.stdout.log:84:   can now be stated with \(\kappa(C)=\delta_*\), avoiding an unresolved LP in the theorem statement.
./logs/iter_009_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:3480:./logs/iter_009_solver.stdout.log:111:Use the explicit \(\delta_*(C)\) formula to attack the known-path anytime characterization. It gives both the optimal local randomized quote law and the exact local obstruction.
