./logs/iter_002_idea_01_explorer_01_critic_01.stderr.log:920:Exploit \(r_t(a)=a\,1\{\max_{s>t}P_s>a\}\). Prices reveal nested threshold information across quotes. Possible payoffs: improved discretization, adaptive grids, or one-sided Lipschitz methods. Keep this separate from delay-tail characterization until the basic theorem is settled.
./logs/iter_002_idea_01_explorer_01_critic_01.stderr.log:932:Formalize the solver's quantifier-collapse observation: if the learner may depend on both the process law and the horizon, every process is learnable by repeatedly playing a horizon-optimal fixed quote up to an epsilon. Then separate the nontrivial regimes: horizon-oblivious process-aware learners, universal learners over a class, minimax learners, and post-history uniform guarantees.
./logs/iter_002_idea_01_explorer_01_critic_01.stderr.log:950:So any nontrivial “only if” theorem must change the quantifier order: e.g. one universal learner over a class, a minimax guarantee, a horizon-oblivious process-aware learner, or a post-history uniform guarantee.
./logs/iter_002_idea_01_explorer_01_critic_01.stderr.log:1011:This also handles the existing maximum-attainment issue: no max is needed, only a supremum and an \(\varepsilon_T\)-optimizer.
./logs/iter_002_idea_01_explorer_01_critic_01.stderr.log:1035:The strict crossing condition still requires `sup`, not `max`, because comparator optima can fail to exist.
./logs/iter_002_idea_01_explorer_01_critic_01.stderr.log:1074:Refuted/weakened: \(\beta\)-necessity, \(\gamma\)-necessity, and \(\Delta_T\)-necessity all fail or are unproved for process-aware instance-level learnability. Comparator `max` is also unsafe under strict crossing; use `sup`.
./logs/iter_002_idea_01_explorer_01_critic_01.stderr.log:1094:- The comparator `max` is unsafe under strict crossing and atoms; future statements should use `sup` or prove attainment.
