./logs/iter_005_idea_02_explorer_01_critic_01.stderr.log:1338:## Quantifiers, Universal Regret, And Minimax
./logs/iter_005_idea_02_explorer_01_critic_01.stderr.log:1340:- Cesa-Bianchi and Lugosi, *Prediction, Learning, and Games* (Cambridge University Press, 2006), verified from the authors' official book page. It is useful for standard online-learning quantifiers: algorithms working for all individual sequences relative to a reference class, expert advice, and minimax regret.
./logs/iter_005_idea_02_explorer_01_critic_01.stderr.log:1342:- Cesa-Bianchi, Gentile, Mansour, and Minora (COLT/PMLR 2016) is now verified. The source explicitly mentions a minimax characterization for bandit learning with delay as a by-product. Relevant lower-bound methodology/background for class-uniform delayed-feedback impossibility, not direct support for the market-making conjecture.
./logs/iter_005_idea_02_explorer_01_critic_01.stderr.log:1357:Existing delayed-learning literature supports sufficiency via bounded, total, adaptive, optimistic, skipped, outstanding-observation, or intermediate-observation delay terms. It does not support necessity of the unweighted \(\beta\) tail. Standard online-learning references also reinforce that the meaningful lower-bound regime is usually universal/minimax or simultaneous-prefix, not horizon- and law-specific oracle policies. Iteration 4's accepted concatenation theorems are direct elementary lower bounds, with the cited unknown-horizon/anytime literature serving as terminology and contrast. The literature is more aligned with aggregate, quantile, best-arm, skipped, value-of-information, horizon-censored, censored-threshold, or adaptive-regret quantities, but the counterexamples show raw delayed reward mass is not necessary for instance-wise learnability unless tied to unpredictability, prefix incompatibility, or information acquisition.
./logs/iter_005_idea_02_explorer_01_critic_01.stderr.log:1372:- Tal Lancewicki, Shahar Segal, Tomer Koren, Yishay Mansour. "Stochastic Multi-Armed Bandits with Unrestricted Delay Distributions." ICML 2021, PMLR 139:5969-5978. https://proceedings.mlr.press/v139/lancewicki21a.html. Relevance: quantile-style control under unbounded or infinite delays.
./logs/iter_005_idea_02_explorer_01_critic_01.stderr.log:1394:- Nicolo Cesa-Bianchi, Claudio Gentile, Yishay Mansour, Alberto Minora. "Delay and Cooperation in Nonstochastic Bandits." 29th Annual Conference on Learning Theory, PMLR 49:605-622, 2016. https://proceedings.mlr.press/v49/cesa-bianchi16.html. Relevance: delayed-bandit minimax background and lower-bound methodology for class-uniform readings.
./logs/iter_005_idea_02_explorer_01_critic_01.stderr.log:1398:- Nicolo Cesa-Bianchi, Gabor Lugosi. *Prediction, Learning, and Games*. Cambridge University Press, 2006. https://cesa-bianchi.di.unimi.it/predbook/. Relevance: standard reference for online prediction, expert advice, individual-sequence guarantees, and minimax regret quantifiers.
./logs/iter_005_idea_02_explorer_01_critic_01.stderr.log:1400:- Andrew C. C. Yao. "Probabilistic Computations: Toward a Unified Measure of Complexity." 18th Annual Symposium on Foundations of Computer Science, 1977, pp. 222-227. DOI: https://doi.org/10.1109/SFCS.1977.24. Relevance: canonical minimax/Yao framing for randomized-algorithm lower bounds; background for two-continuation lower-bound interpretation.
./logs/iter_005_idea_02_explorer_01_critic_01.stderr.log:1432:- Haipeng Luo, Robert E. Schapire. "Towards Minimax Online Learning with Unknown Time Horizon." Proceedings of the 31st International Conference on Machine Learning, PMLR 32(1):226-234, 2014. https://proceedings.mlr.press/v32/luo14.html. Relevance: background for fixed-horizon, horizon-aware, and unknown/adversarial-horizon quantifier distinctions.
