./logs/iter_003_idea_02_explorer_01.stderr.log:929:Let \(s_n=2^n\), \(P_{s_n}=U_n/s_n\), \(P_t=0\) otherwise. Then at \(t=s_n\), \(a=0\) has \(\tau_{s_n}(0)=s_{n+1}-s_n=2^n\) almost surely, so \(\beta(h)=1\) for all \(h\). For fixed \(a>0\), if \(m^*=\max\{m:s_m\le T,\ a<1/s_m\}\) exists, only \(t<s_{m^*}\) can earn and \(\mathbb E\sum_t r_t(a)\le a s_{m^*}<1\); if it does not exist, reward is zero. Thus \(R_T\le1\) for the always-zero learner. Status: accepted.
./logs/iter_003_idea_02_explorer_01.stderr.log:981:An epsilon version comparing to \(1/2-\eta\) and \(1-\eta\) gives \(N(1/4-\eta)\). Status: accepted for universal/minimax lower-bound use.
./logs/iter_003_idea_02_explorer_01.stderr.log:996:Failed or strongly weakened. Predictable sparse spikes can make \(\Delta_T(H_T)=\Omega(T)\) along infinitely many horizons for every \(H_T\log T=o(T)\), while a process-aware learner posts a known near-optimal quote and gets sublinear regret. Raw delayed reward mass is not the same as information needed for learning.
./logs/iter_003_idea_02_explorer_01.stderr.log:998:## FA-004: Using Comparator `max` Without Checking Attainment
./logs/iter_003_idea_02_explorer_01.stderr.log:1020:Failed under the current expected-comparator regret definition. Randomizing the terminal continuation \(c\in\{1/2,1\}\) is useful as a minimax/Yao-style proof device for deterministic instances, but the stochastic mixture itself may be easy because the comparator is \(\sup_a \mathbb E[\text{reward of }a]\). A learner can often match the best expected fixed quote up to epsilon. The block construction should be stated as a universal-class or minimax lower bound, not as an instance-wise stochastic-process lower bound.
