./logs/iter_003_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:1054:Existing delayed-learning literature supports sufficiency via bounded, total, adaptive, optimistic, or skipped delay terms. It does not support necessity of the unweighted \(\beta\) tail. Standard online-learning references also reinforce that the meaningful lower-bound regime is usually universal or minimax, not horizon- and law-specific oracle policies. The literature is more aligned with aggregate, quantile, best-arm, skipped, value-of-information, or horizon-censored delay quantities, but the iteration's counterexamples show even raw delayed reward mass is not necessary for instance-wise learnability unless tied to unpredictability or information acquisition.
