./logs/iter_008_synthesis.stderr.log:1738:The zero-block threshold pair \(\{1/2,1\}\) has positive local compatibility gap \(1/4\), but this alone does not force endpoint regret in a larger process. If, after the threshold is revealed, there is a long suffix in which the learner can exploit the revealed environment and earn enough reward, the local prefix deficit can be offset unless the theorem caps post-divergence learner surplus. Similarly, if the hard common prefix occurs only on a branch of probability \(p_n\ll1\), the conditional gap may stay large while its expected contribution is only \(p_n |I|\kappa\).
./logs/iter_008_synthesis.stderr.log:1783:- Lancewicki, Segal, Koren, and Mansour (ICML/PMLR 2021) studies stochastic bandits with unrestricted and infinite delays, with quantile-based delay dependence. Verified source; relevant for quantile alternatives to uniform tails.
./logs/iter_008_synthesis.stderr.log:1791:- Schlisselberg, Lancewicki, Auer, and Mansour (NeurIPS 2025/arXiv:2505.24193) is verified. It gives best-of-both-worlds delayed-bandit bounds with terms involving cumulative delay and maximum missing observations. Useful for adaptive-delay context, but not direct support for the public-price full-information market-making reduction.
./logs/iter_008_synthesis.stderr.log:1793:- Masoudian, Zimmert, and Seldin (NeurIPS/OpenReview 2022/arXiv:2206.14906) is verified as the earlier best-of-both-worlds delayed-bandit paper. It includes arbitrary-delay bounds using total delay, maximal delay, and outstanding observations, and is the predecessor to the 2024 robustness-to-excessive-delays work already in the map.
./logs/iter_008_synthesis.stderr.log:1800:- Duetting, Guruganesh, Schneider, and Wang (ICML/PMLR 2023) is now verified. It studies one-sided Lipschitz maximization, motivated by pricing/contract design, and gives very small regret in that setting. Potential tool for threshold reward geometry and improved discretization, but delayed/adversarial time variation may block direct application.
./logs/iter_008_synthesis.stderr.log:1813:- Bergemann and Schlag (JEEA 2008) is verified. It studies minimax/regret pricing without priors and is relevant robust-pricing background for primal action-law ideas, not a proof of CL-023.
./logs/iter_008_synthesis.stderr.log:1814:- Chen, Hu, and Wang (Operations Research 2024) is verified. It gives robust screening/minimax-pricing duality adjacent to the \(\kappa\) LP viewpoint.
./logs/iter_008_synthesis.stderr.log:1818:## Quantifiers, Universal Regret, And Minimax
./logs/iter_008_synthesis.stderr.log:1820:- Cesa-Bianchi and Lugosi, *Prediction, Learning, and Games* (Cambridge University Press, 2006), verified from the authors' official book page. It is useful for standard online-learning quantifiers: algorithms working for all individual sequences relative to a reference class, expert advice, and minimax regret.
./logs/iter_008_synthesis.stderr.log:1822:- Cesa-Bianchi, Gentile, Mansour, and Minora (COLT/PMLR 2016) is now verified. The source explicitly mentions a minimax characterization for bandit learning with delay as a by-product. Relevant lower-bound methodology/background for class-uniform delayed-feedback impossibility, not direct support for the market-making conjecture.
./logs/iter_008_synthesis.stderr.log:1829:- Blackwell (Pacific J. Math. 1956) and Sion (Pacific J. Math. 1958) are verified as classical approachability/minimax references. They are relevant only as conceptual/minimax support for the prefix-compatibility LP; the market-making transfer still needs a direct proof.
./logs/iter_008_synthesis.stderr.log:1842:Existing delayed-learning literature supports sufficiency via bounded, total, adaptive, optimistic, skipped, outstanding-observation, or intermediate-observation delay terms. It does not support necessity of the unweighted \(\beta\) tail. Standard online-learning references also reinforce that the meaningful lower-bound regime is usually universal/minimax or simultaneous-prefix, not horizon- and law-specific oracle policies. Iteration 4's accepted concatenation theorems are direct elementary lower bounds, with the cited unknown-horizon/anytime literature serving as terminology and contrast. The literature is more aligned with aggregate, quantile, best-arm, skipped, value-of-information, horizon-censored, censored-threshold, or adaptive-regret quantities, but the counterexamples show raw delayed reward mass is not necessary for instance-wise learnability unless tied to unpredictability, prefix incompatibility, or information acquisition.
./logs/iter_008_synthesis.stderr.log:1846:Iteration 7 sharpens the internal threshold geometry: the exact \(m\)-positive-support local gap is \(((m-1)/m)^m\), approaching \(1/e\). The new price-discrimination/robust-pricing sources above are useful context for equal-revenue and minimax duality, but the finite-support constant and the terminal-block \(1/e\) universal lift should currently be cited as internal results, not as imported theorems.
./logs/iter_008_synthesis.stderr.log:1863:- Tal Lancewicki, Shahar Segal, Tomer Koren, Yishay Mansour. "Stochastic Multi-Armed Bandits with Unrestricted Delay Distributions." ICML 2021, PMLR 139:5969-5978. https://proceedings.mlr.press/v139/lancewicki21a.html. Relevance: quantile-style control under unbounded or infinite delays.
./logs/iter_008_synthesis.stderr.log:1885:- Nicolo Cesa-Bianchi, Claudio Gentile, Yishay Mansour, Alberto Minora. "Delay and Cooperation in Nonstochastic Bandits." 29th Annual Conference on Learning Theory, PMLR 49:605-622, 2016. https://proceedings.mlr.press/v49/cesa-bianchi16.html. Relevance: delayed-bandit minimax background and lower-bound methodology for class-uniform readings.
./logs/iter_008_synthesis.stderr.log:1889:- Nicolo Cesa-Bianchi, Gabor Lugosi. *Prediction, Learning, and Games*. Cambridge University Press, 2006. https://cesa-bianchi.di.unimi.it/predbook/. Relevance: standard reference for online prediction, expert advice, individual-sequence guarantees, and minimax regret quantifiers.
./logs/iter_008_synthesis.stderr.log:1891:- Andrew C. C. Yao. "Probabilistic Computations: Toward a Unified Measure of Complexity." 18th Annual Symposium on Foundations of Computer Science, 1977, pp. 222-227. DOI: https://doi.org/10.1109/SFCS.1977.24. Relevance: canonical minimax/Yao framing for randomized-algorithm lower bounds; background for two-continuation lower-bound interpretation.
./logs/iter_008_synthesis.stderr.log:1923:- Haipeng Luo, Robert E. Schapire. "Towards Minimax Online Learning with Unknown Time Horizon." Proceedings of the 31st International Conference on Machine Learning, PMLR 32(1):226-234, 2014. https://proceedings.mlr.press/v32/luo14.html. Relevance: background for fixed-horizon, horizon-aware, and unknown/adversarial-horizon quantifier distinctions.
./logs/iter_008_synthesis.stderr.log:1935:- Saeed Masoudian, Julian Zimmert, Yevgeny Seldin. "A Best-of-Both-Worlds Algorithm for Bandits with Delayed Feedback." NeurIPS 2022 / arXiv:2206.14906. https://arxiv.org/abs/2206.14906. Relevance: arbitrary-delay bandit bounds using total delay, maximal delay, and outstanding observations; predecessor to the 2024 robustness-to-excessive-delays paper.
./logs/iter_008_synthesis.stderr.log:1943:- David Blackwell. "An analog of the minimax theorem for vector payoffs." Pacific Journal of Mathematics 6(1):1-8, 1956. https://msp.org/pjm/1956/6-1/p01.xhtml. Relevance: classical origin of approachability/vector-payoff feasibility; conceptual background for prefix-compatibility formulations.
./logs/iter_008_synthesis.stderr.log:1945:- Maurice Sion. "On general minimax theorems." Pacific Journal of Mathematics 8(1):171-176, 1958. https://msp.org/pjm/1958/8-1/p14.xhtml. Relevance: direct minimax theorem background for finite-grid LP duality; not by itself a market-making lower-bound proof.
./logs/iter_008_synthesis.stderr.log:1973:- Dirk Bergemann, Karl H. Schlag. "Pricing Without Priors." *Journal of the European Economic Association* 6(2-3):560-569, 2008. DOI: https://doi.org/10.1162/JEEA.2008.6.2-3.560. Relevance: minimax/regret pricing without distributional priors; useful robust-pricing background for primal action-law ideas around \(\kappa\).
./logs/iter_008_synthesis.stderr.log:1975:- Zhi Chen, Zhenyu Hu, Ruiqin Wang. "Screening with Limited Information: A Dual Perspective." *Operations Research* 72(4):1487-1504, 2024. DOI: https://doi.org/10.1287/opre.2022.0016. Relevance: robust screening and minimax pricing duality; adjacent to the LP-duality interpretation of the multi-threshold \(\kappa\) gap.
