./iterations/iter_003/consolidation.md:8:Raw \(\beta\)-necessity is still false, even for horizon-oblivious policies, because sparse vanishing spikes admit an anytime always-zero learner. The infinite concatenation arguments were not promoted to proved theorems. Stochastic mixtures remain unsafe under \(\sup_a \mathbb E-\mathbb E\) pseudo-regret.
./iterations/iter_008/consolidation.md:2:- Promoted CL-026/LB-020: same-path multi-threshold anytime lift. For increasing block \(0^N,c_1,\ldots,c_m\), horizon-oblivious policies satisfy \(\max_j R_{M+N+j}\ge N\kappa(C)-M\).
./iterations/iter_010/solution_critic_01.md:3:The main derandomization idea looks mathematically plausible. I did not find a counterexample to the monotone-threshold uniform convergence lemma. The proof can likely be made rigorous by an elementary grid/bracketing plus Hoeffding plus Borel-Cantelli argument.
./iterations/iter_010/solution_critic_01.md:9:- If the claim is “the original iff/tail characterization is solved,” this is a fatal mismatch. The attempt explicitly leaves the original iff false and proves only a deterministic derandomization of the known-path horizon-oblivious \(1/e\) upper bound.
./iterations/iter_010/solution_critic_01.md:10:- If the claim is only the derandomization theorem, I found no fatal mathematical flaw in the core argument.
./iterations/iter_010/solution_critic_01.md:16:- The regret comparator should be `sup`, not `max`.
./iterations/iter_010/solution_critic_01.md:27:- “Borel-Cantelli yields an infinite deterministic realization” is only sketched. It is probably true, but the proof needs the actual failure probability:
./iterations/iter_010/solution_critic_01.md:36:- The monotone-threshold derandomization lemma is credible. For fixed grid-valued monotone \(d_1\ge\cdots\ge d_n\), Hoeffding applies to independent bounded variables
./iterations/iter_010/solution_critic_01.md:40:  The number of grid-valued monotone paths is \(\binom{n+m}{m}\), so a union bound is subexponential for \(m=o(n)\).
./iterations/iter_010/solution_critic_01.md:46:- Applying the lemma to \(M_{t,T}=\max_{t<s\le T}P_s\) is valid because \(M_{1,T}\ge\cdots\ge M_{T-1,T}\).
./iterations/iter_010/solution_critic_01.md:50:I found no direct delayed-market-making theorem matching this deterministic derandomization. The argument is best treated as an internal elementary probabilistic-method result.
./iterations/iter_010/solution_critic_01.md:52:Relevant background is empirical-process/probabilistic-method and low-discrepancy literature, not delayed-feedback learning. The delayed-feedback sources remain relevant to earlier sufficiency work but not to this derandomization.
./iterations/iter_010/solution_critic_01.md:58:- Aad W. van der Vaart and Jon A. Wellner. *Weak Convergence and Empirical Processes*, Springer, 1996. https://link.springer.com/book/10.1007/978-1-4757-2545-2. Relevant as general background for uniform convergence over indexed function classes; the current proof uses only a finite bracketing special case.
./iterations/iter_010/solution_critic_01.md:62:- V. N. Vapnik and A. Ya. Chervonenkis. “On the Uniform Convergence of Relative Frequencies of Events to Their Probabilities.” *Theory of Probability and Its Applications*, 1971. DOI: https://doi.org/10.1137/1116025. Relevant background for uniform convergence, though the solver’s finite monotone-grid union bound is more direct.
./iterations/iter_002/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:25:- **Missing assumption:** No computability or statistical access constraints are imposed. The proof may choose a nonconstructive approximate maximizer of \(V_T^P\). This is fine for existence, not for an algorithmic theorem.
./iterations/iter_002/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:49:- The use of `sup` and \(\varepsilon\)-optimizers is correct and avoids the known strict-crossing maximum-attainment pathology.
./iterations/iter_002/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:63:3. Decide whether the next nontrivial regime is horizon-oblivious process-aware, universal over a class, minimax over a class, or post-history conditional regret.
./iterations/iter_003/solution_critic_01.md:5:But the writeup is not acceptable as a full solution/characterization. It mixes three different claims: a sufficient upper bound, an instance-wise counterexample, and a possible universal/minimax lower bound. Only the first two are substantially supported; the lower-bound claim remains a sketch.
./iterations/iter_003/solution_critic_01.md:10:   The attempt says the iff is false instance-wise, then discusses a universal-class lower bound. These are different theorems. A final solution must state whether learners are process-aware, horizon-aware, universal over a class, minimax, or oracle policies.
./iterations/iter_003/solution_critic_01.md:13:   The finite \(0^N c\) gadget is sound, but the extension to infinitely many dominant blocks needs a formal deterministic construction, conditioning on randomized learner histories, horizon-aware diagonalization if applicable, and exact accounting for pending rewards from earlier blocks.
./iterations/iter_003/solution_critic_01.md:22:- Regret should use \(\sup_{a\in[0,1]}\), not \(\max\), unless comparator attainment is proved.
./iterations/iter_003/solution_critic_01.md:30:- The universal-class block lower bound “supports minimax/universal necessity” only after a missing concatenation proof.
./iterations/iter_003/solution_critic_01.md:45:- The finite two-continuation block lower bound is credible; only the infinite concatenation remains incomplete.
./iterations/iter_004/promising_ideas.json:1:{"bibliography_candidates":[{"authors":"Julius Graf, Thibaut Mastrolia","relevance":"Recent adjacent market-making work with closing-auction/terminal-liquidity effects; useful for terminology but not a direct delayed-fill characterization result.","title":"Learning Market Making with Closing Auctions","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.17247","venue_year":"arXiv:2601.17247, 2026"},{"authors":"Ofir Schlisselberg, Tal Lancewicki, Peter Auer, Yishay Mansour","relevance":"Modern delayed-bandit bounds matching lower bounds and emphasizing cumulative delay/missing observations, relevant to adaptive truncation alternatives.","title":"Improved Best-of-Both-Worlds Regret for Bandits with Delayed Feedback","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.24193","venue_year":"NeurIPS 2025 / arXiv:2505.24193"},{"authors":"Saeed Masoudian, Julian Zimmert, Yevgeny Seldin","relevance":"Robust delayed-bandit algorithm using skipping and outstanding-observation counts; conceptually useful for replacing hard tail cutoffs by missing-information penalties.","title":"A Best-of-both-worlds Algorithm for Bandits with Delayed Feedback with Robustness to Excessive Delays","url":"https://openreview.net/forum?id=LDzrQB4X5w","venue_year":"NeurIPS 2024"},{"authors":"Haipeng Luo, Robert E. Schapire","relevance":"Directly relevant background for unknown/adversarial horizon quantifiers and horizon-aware versus horizon-oblivious online learning.","title":"Towards Minimax Online Learning with Unknown Time Horizon","url":"https://proceedings.mlr.press/v32/luo14.html","venue_year":"ICML 2014, PMLR 32(1):226-234"},{"authors":"Eyal Gofer, Yishay Mansour","relevance":"Studies individual-sequence anytime regret over prefixes; useful for positioning the same-path prefix-conflict lower bound.","title":"Lower bounds on individual sequence regret","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s10994-015-5531-y","venue_year":"Machine Learning 103(1):1-26, 2016"},{"authors":"Cristobal Guzman, Nishant Mehta, Ali Mortazavi","relevance":"Clarifies best-case and anytime-prefix notions in online learning; useful for avoiding quantifier confusion in prefix lower bounds.","title":"Best-case lower bounds in online learning","url":"https://proceedings.neurips.cc/paper/2021/hash/b7da6669894867f04b8727876a69ffc0-Abstract.html","venue_year":"NeurIPS 2021"},{"authors":"Shie Mannor, John N. Tsitsiklis, Jia Yuan Yu","relevance":"Sample-path constraint framework may help formulate simultaneous-prefix compatibility or approachability-style feasibility conditions.","title":"Online Learning with Sample Path Constraints","url":"https://jmlr.org/beta/papers/v10/mannor09a.html","venue_year":"JMLR 10:569-590, 2009"}],"ideas":[{"id":"idea_01","risks":"Quantifier mistakes are the main risk: process-unaware, horizon-aware, randomized, and adaptive-adversary variants differ. Cross-block pending rewards and strict-threshold suprema also need careful epsilon accounting.","suggested_lenses":["proof strategy","quantifier audit","counterexample search"],"summary":"Turn the solver's concatenated universal block sketch into a theorem. For any randomized learner that does not know future continuations, choose dominant blocks 0^{N_i} c_i with c_i in {1/2,1}, M_i=o(N_i), and prove limsup_T R_T/T >= 1/4. Check the coupling after a fixed prefix, the old-reward cap by M_i, the eta-comparator version for strict crossing, and beta/gamma failure on the constructed sequence.","title":"Formalize Universal Block Lower Bound","why_promising":"The finite two-continuation lemma and prefix-cap fragment are already accepted; this is the shortest path from solver sketch to a real class-uniform impossibility theorem."},{"id":"idea_02","risks":"The policy model must be exact: a process-aware policy may know the entire infinite path but not the evaluation horizon. Need ensure later blocks cannot retroactively affect regret at the paired endpoints.","suggested_lenses":["proof strategy","model definition","edge-case audit"],"summary":"Formalize the same-path horizon-oblivious theorem for the deterministic path made of blocks 0^{N_i}, 1/2, 1. Prove that for endpoints T_i^0=M_i+N_i+1 and T_i^1=M_i+N_i+2, any horizon-oblivious policy satisfies max{R_{T_i^0},R_{T_i^1}} >= N_i(1/4-o(1))-M_i-O(1).","title":"Known-Path Horizon-Oblivious Obstruction","why_promising":"It cleanly separates the horizon-aware oracle collapse from anytime learning, using one fixed deterministic process rather than a process class."},{"id":"idea_03","risks":"A family pi_T may be allowed to change its current-block behavior at each endpoint, so the infinite construction may require a diagonal or endpoint-specific adversarial choice. The result may only hold for oblivious adversaries or for fixed algorithms.","suggested_lenses":["quantifier audit","proof strategy"],"summary":"Investigate whether the universal block lower bound extends to horizon-aware learner families pi_T that know T but not the continuation sequence. The finite two-continuation gadget has the same horizon under c=1/2 and c=1, suggesting an endpoint-wise construction may work; write or refute the diagonalization carefully.","title":"Horizon-Aware Universal Learners","why_promising":"Many regret statements allow the horizon as input. Settling this would make the lower-bound regime much clearer than the current anytime-only statement."},{"id":"idea_04","risks":"The quantity may become tautological, equivalent to minimax regret itself, or too hard to estimate. A useful definition must explain CE-001 as easy despite beta failure.","suggested_lenses":["definition design","lower-bound strategy","counterexample search"],"summary":"Define a decision-relevant prefix-incompatibility modulus: over pairs of continuations or same-path horizon pairs sharing a public prefix, take the smallest possible worst regret of any common-prefix action rule. Test whether finite blocks give modulus about N/4 while sparse vanishing spikes have small modulus despite beta(h)=1.","title":"Prefix-Incompatibility Tail Substitute","why_promising":"Current evidence says delay length is not the obstruction; incompatible optimal prefixes before resolving information are. A modulus of this kind could become the right necessity statement."},{"id":"idea_05","risks":"Positive-payoff rare branches may still create nonnegligible comparator value unless probabilities decay fast enough. Essential-sup tails require positive-probability branches at all large delays, and all-horizon regret bounds are easy to get wrong.","suggested_lenses":["counterexample construction","horizon accounting"],"summary":"Build a complete rare-history process where beta or gamma fails by essential supremum on tiny positive-probability branches, while expected pseudo-regret is o(T) for a simple process-aware or universal learner. Compute both conditional tails and comparator values for all horizons.","title":"Rare-History Essential-Sup Stress Test","why_promising":"It would show that even positive-scale conditional tail conditions can be too strong under expected pseudo-regret, strengthening the case for averaged or information-based quantities."},{"id":"idea_06","risks":"Most cited algorithms are bandit/OCO rather than public-price full-information threshold rewards. The market-making reduction must specify exactly when each grid arm's reward is known and how skipped fills affect comparator regret.","suggested_lenses":["literature mapping","algorithm design","proof strategy"],"summary":"Translate adaptive skipping and outstanding-observation delayed-learning results to the grid market-making problem. Aim for a sufficient theorem that does not require a known H and is bounded by skipped payoff mass or value-weighted outstanding rewards, not by sup_h beta(h).","title":"Adaptive Skipping Sufficiency","why_promising":"Recent delayed-feedback literature increasingly treats missing information, not elapsed time, as the complexity measure; this matches the counterexamples where long delays are predictable or irrelevant."},{"id":"idea_07","risks":"The delayed setting may still require many active breakpoints, and improved discretization will not address necessity. Strict crossing and atoms mean exact maximizers may still fail without left-limit conventions.","suggested_lenses":["structural analysis","algorithm design"],"summary":"Exploit the identity r_t^H(a)=a 1{M_t^H>a}, where M_t^H is the future-window maximum price. Study whether the continuum comparator can be handled exactly by intervals between observed prices, left limits, or adaptive grids, reducing or removing the T/K discretization term in the sufficiency theorem.","title":"Threshold Geometry For Continuum Quotes","why_promising":"It could turn the sufficient theorem into a cleaner continuum result and clarify sup-versus-max pathologies without relying on a crude uniform grid."}],"solution_status":"unsolved"}
./iterations/iter_003/solver_attempt.md:4:As written, the iff conjecture is false. Assumption \(\beta(h)\to0\) is a valid sufficient condition under the public/exogenous full-information interpretation, but it is not necessary for instance-wise pseudo-regret. A true necessity theorem still needs stronger quantifiers, such as universal/minimax learning over a class.
./iterations/iter_003/solver_attempt.md:41:so \(\limsup_T R_T/T\ge 1/4\). This supports minimax/universal necessity, not instance-wise necessity.
./iterations/iter_003/solver_attempt.md:46:The finite-grid upper bound also needs explicit assumptions: public exogenous prices, counterfactual grid rewards observable after truncation delay, and regret against \(\sup_a\), not \(\max_a\).
./iterations/iter_003/solver_attempt.md:62:Formalize the concatenated block lower bound as a theorem for universal/minimax learning.
./iterations/iter_003/solver_attempt.md:73:Use \(\sup\), not \(\max\), everywhere unless comparator attainment is proved.
./iterations/iter_003/promising_ideas.json:1:{"bibliography_candidates":[{"authors":"Emmanuel Esposito, Saeed Masoudian, Hao Qiu, Dirk van der Hoeven, Nicolò Cesa-Bianchi, Yevgeny Seldin","relevance":"Intermediate observations are a close analogue of public price history that may predict delayed fills; useful for formalizing when predictable tails cease to be harmful.","title":"Delayed Bandits: When Do Intermediate Observations Help?","url":"https://proceedings.mlr.press/v202/esposito23a.html","venue_year":"ICML/PMLR 2023"},{"authors":"Ciara Pike-Burke, Shipra Agrawal, Csaba Szepesvari, Steffen Grunewalder","relevance":"Now verified from PMLR; relevant background for delayed/aggregated feedback and for distinguishing identifiable full-information fills from harder anonymous feedback.","title":"Bandits with Delayed, Aggregated Anonymous Feedback","url":"https://proceedings.mlr.press/v80/pike-burke18a.html","venue_year":"ICML/PMLR 2018"},{"authors":"Marcelo J. Weinberger, Erik Ordentlich","relevance":"Older full-information delayed prediction reference already listed as unchecked; DBLP/DOI metadata verify bibliographic details and support the delayed-experts background section.","title":"On delayed prediction of individual sequences","url":"https://doi.org/10.1109/TIT.2002.1013136","venue_year":"IEEE Transactions on Information Theory 2002"},{"authors":"Alexander Ryabchenko, Idan Attias, Daniel M. Roy","relevance":"Very recent reduction-style delayed OCO paper with total-delay and outstanding-observation terms; may inform adaptive truncation/oracle inequalities beyond fixed H.","title":"A Reduction from Delayed to Immediate Feedback for Online Convex Optimization with Improved Guarantees","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.02634","venue_year":"arXiv 2026"},{"authors":"Jiahao Zhang, Tao Lin, Weiqiang Zheng, Zhe Feng, Yifeng Teng, Xiaotie Deng","relevance":"Threshold learning with censored feedback and one-sided Lipschitz assumptions; potentially useful for the continuous quote geometry and limits of discretization-free methods.","title":"Learning Thresholds with Latent Values and Censored Feedback","url":"https://openreview.net/forum?id=qaKRfobbTg","venue_year":"ICLR 2024"},{"authors":"Jianyu Xu, Yining Wang, Xi Chen, Yu-Xiang Wang","relevance":"Recent dynamic pricing paper with adversarial censoring; adjacent terminology and techniques for censored observations, though not a direct delayed-fill characterization.","title":"Dynamic Pricing with Adversarially-Censored Demands","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.06168","venue_year":"arXiv 2025, revised 2026"}],"ideas":[{"id":"idea_01","risks":"The comparator over the whole prefix is a single fixed quote, so cross-block rewards and previous pending quotes can invalidate a naive per-block regret decomposition. Horizon-aware learner families may require an explicit diagonalization argument.","suggested_lenses":["proof strategy","lower bound formalization","randomized learner conditioning"],"summary":"Turn CL-016 into a precise theorem. Fix a universal process-unaware learner, choose dominant deterministic blocks 0^{N_i} c_i with c_i in {1/2,1} and M_i=o(N_i), condition on the fixed prefix and the first N_i zeros, use the finite two-continuation lemma to choose c_i causing at least N_i/4 current-block regret, then prove earlier blocks and pending rewards cost only O(M_i). State separately for anytime learners and horizon-indexed learner families.","title":"Prove the concatenated universal block lower bound","why_promising":"This is the closest current route to a genuine necessity-style statement: it converts the accepted finite indistinguishability gadget into a class-uniform impossibility without reviving the false instance-wise claim."},{"id":"idea_02","risks":"The direction may collapse too: known-law anytime learners might be able to schedule near-optimal quotes by doubling or by Blackwell-style calibration, making no tail condition necessary even without horizon knowledge. Conversely, examples may depend on artificial noncomputability or rapidly changing horizon optima.","suggested_lenses":["quantifier audit","counterexample search","online-to-batch reduction"],"summary":"Investigate the regime between oracle collapse and universal learning: the learner knows the exogenous process law but not the horizon, and must use one anytime policy with pseudo-regret o(T) for every prefix. Either prove a generic anytime collapse via grids/doubling over horizon-optimal fixed quotes, or construct a known process whose horizon-optimal quote switches across dominant horizons so that no single quote sequence tracks all prefixes.","title":"Horizon-oblivious process-aware learnability","why_promising":"The notes identify horizon awareness as the fatal reason instance-wise necessity is vacuous. Removing only that assumption is a clean, separable quantifier change that may yield the weakest nontrivial necessity theorem."},{"id":"idea_03","risks":"A proposed modulus can become tautological if it merely restates minimax regret. It must produce either an upper-bound algorithm or a lower-bound lemma with checkable hypotheses on histories, continuation values, and payoff scales.","suggested_lenses":["definition design","Le Cam/Yao lower bounds","upper-lower matching"],"summary":"Define a decision-relevant delayed-tail or value-of-information quantity for a process class: after a history, measure pairs of continuations that remain observationally identical until after H but have separated optimal fixed quotes or separated comparator values. Prove a two-continuation lower bound in terms of this separation and compare it with sufficient quantities Γ_T(H), Δ_T(H), and skipped payoff mass.","title":"Replace raw tails by an information obstruction","why_promising":"All accepted counterexamples show that long tails alone are harmless when low-payoff, sparse, or predictable. A continuation-separation modulus directly targets the missing ingredient: unpredictability that affects the optimal quote."},{"id":"idea_04","risks":"Pathwise regret is a different problem, so it cannot be presented as a fix to the original pseudo-regret theorem without explicit restatement. Sparse vanishing-payoff examples may still refute unweighted β-necessity, requiring a weighted or value-based tail instead.","suggested_lenses":["regret notion comparison","stochastic mixture lower bound","counterexample transfer"],"summary":"Analyze the alternative regret E[sup_a sum_t r_t(a) - sum_t r_t(a_t)]. Revisit the random two-continuation block under this pathwise comparator; unlike pseudo-regret, the hidden terminal state should remain hard. Then test whether β, γ, or an information-weighted tail becomes necessary under this stronger hindsight comparator.","title":"Pathwise comparator variant","why_promising":"The oracle collapse and stochastic-mixture warning are specific to sup_a E[reward]. If the original motivation wanted hindsight over realized prices, this variant may restore a meaningful tail characterization."},{"id":"idea_05","risks":"Combining many truncation horizons can introduce extra logarithmic factors and feedback alignment problems; rewards for different H correspond to different surrogate games. Existing bandit/OCO skipping results may not transfer directly despite public prices.","suggested_lenses":["algorithm design","oracle inequality","literature mapping"],"summary":"Build an adaptive finite-grid algorithm that does not require a known H or known β decay. Target an oracle bound like inf_H {sqrt(T(H+1) log K)+T/K+Γ_T(H)} plus polylog factors, using multi-scale truncation, sleeping experts over H, or skip-set reductions. Map Joulani et al. 2013/2016, Gyorgy-Joulani 2021, Flaspohler et al. 2021, and Ryabchenko-Attias-Roy 2026 to the public-price full-information setting.","title":"Adaptive truncation and skipping oracle bounds","why_promising":"This would strengthen the accepted sufficiency theorem from a hand-tuned H(T) proof to a practical anytime theorem and may identify the right algorithmic tail penalty."},{"id":"idea_06","risks":"The expected contribution must stay small for all horizons while the essential supremum remains large on positive-probability histories. It is easy to accidentally create linear comparator value or a process-aware optimal quote that does not actually achieve small regret.","suggested_lenses":["counterexample construction","horizon calculations","essential-sup stress test"],"summary":"Finish CE-006 by constructing a rare-branch process: with tiny probability p_n reveal a code leading to a long positive-payoff delay L_n, with p_n L_n summable or sublinear over horizons, while conditional on the rare code the tail probability is one for arbitrarily large h. Compute β, γ, Δ_T, Γ_T, and pseudo-regret for a simple learner.","title":"Rare-history essential-sup counterexample","why_promising":"It would separate uniform conditional tail assumptions from ex-ante learnability even at positive payoff scale, clarifying whether γ fails because of predictability, rarity, or both."},{"id":"idea_07","risks":"The reward functions a 1{threshold>a} have discontinuities, so one-sided Lipschitz tools may require distributional smoothness absent from the adversarial/public-price model. The standard T/K downward grid bound may already be minimax without extra assumptions.","suggested_lenses":["threshold geometry","discretization lower bounds","smoothness assumptions"],"summary":"Explore whether threshold/censored-feedback geometry can improve or qualify the grid discretization step. Study f_t^H(a)=a 1{M_t^H>a}, where M_t^H=max_{s≤H} P_{t+s}, and ask whether adaptive partitions, one-sided Lipschitz assumptions, or censored-threshold results yield better continuum regret than T/K, or prove an adversarial lower bound showing the grid loss is unavoidable.","title":"Continuous quote geometry beyond uniform grids","why_promising":"This is independent of tail necessity and could sharpen the sufficiency theorem. The newly checked threshold/censored papers give terminology and possible assumptions for a non-adversarial refinement."}],"solution_status":"unsolved"}
./iterations/iter_002/idea_01/explorer_01.md:5:So any nontrivial “only if” theorem must change the quantifier order: e.g. one universal learner over a class, a minimax guarantee, a horizon-oblivious process-aware learner, or a post-history uniform guarantee.
./iterations/iter_002/idea_01/explorer_01.md:66:This also handles the existing maximum-attainment issue: no max is needed, only a supremum and an \(\varepsilon_T\)-optimizer.
./iterations/iter_002/idea_01/explorer_01.md:90:The strict crossing condition still requires `sup`, not `max`, because comparator optima can fail to exist.
./iterations/iter_002/idea_02/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:5:It does not prove the original instance-wise “only if” direction. It proves, at most, a class-uniform/minimax obstruction for a learner that does not know the realized deterministic terminal-bit sequence.
./iterations/iter_002/idea_02/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:19:- **Unsupported citation relevance:** Joulani et al. and Quanrud-Khashabi support delayed-feedback upper-bound context, not this lower bound. Yao is relevant only for minimax framing; the finite two-instance averaging proof is already direct.
./iterations/iter_002/idea_02/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:35:No conflict found. The delayed-feedback literature supports sufficiency-style regret controls, not necessity of raw tail decay. Yao’s minimax principle is an appropriate background citation for distributional-vs-randomized lower-bound framing, but the block proof itself is elementary averaging over two continuations.
./iterations/iter_002/idea_02/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:51:- Andrew C. C. Yao. “Probabilistic Computations: Toward a Unified Measure of Complexity.” FOCS 1977, pp. 222-227. DOI: https://doi.org/10.1109/SFCS.1977.24. Relevance: canonical minimax/Yao framing for turning hard input distributions into randomized-algorithm lower bounds; useful background, though the finite-block proof here is direct.
./iterations/iter_010/idea_02/idea.md:6:Reformulate the derandomization lemma as a uniform law of large numbers for the class f_c(t,a)=a 1{a<c_t}, where c_t is nonincreasing in t. Try to prove the needed uniform convergence via bracketing entropy for monotone functions or bounded-variation classes, and compare the resulting rate to the solver's finite-grid union bound.
./iterations/iter_010/idea_02/idea.md:9:A reusable entropy proof would be cleaner than an ad hoc grid argument and may also support infinite-menu or nonstationary variants later.
./iterations/iter_010/idea_02/idea.md:12:Standard empirical-process theorems usually assume iid observations, while here the time coordinates are fixed and the threshold sequence is triangular. The monotone-boundary class may need a custom bracketing argument rather than direct citation.
./iterations/iter_010/idea_02/idea.md:15:- empirical process
./iterations/iter_010/idea_02/idea.md:16:- entropy bounds
./iterations/iter_006/consolidation.md:5:\kappa(C)=\max_{\lambda\in\Delta(C)}\left(\mathbb E_\lambda C-\sup_a a\Pr_\lambda(C>a)\right),
./iterations/iter_006/idea_02/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:3:The explorer’s transfer proof is worth pursuing, but only as a conditional lower-bound lemma. The algebra is mostly sound once the hypotheses are explicit. It is not a characterization and it does not apply automatically to continuous-action learners, process-aware learners, rare events, or suffix-compensated instances.
./iterations/iter_006/idea_02/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:15:  \max_u R_u\ge p|I|\kappa_I-B
./iterations/iter_006/idea_02/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:47:- Suffix-compensation test: append a long post-divergence suffix where the learner can earn enough after the candidate is revealed. Positive local \(\kappa\) alone does not force endpoint regret.
./iterations/iter_006/idea_02/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:51:No direct conflict found. Sion’s minimax theorem supports the finite convex minimax step, but finite zero-sum game duality is already enough. Blackwell is useful background for approachability/vector-payoff framing, not direct support for this market-making transfer.
./iterations/iter_006/idea_02/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:63:- The lemma is useful for universal/minimax and horizon-oblivious prefix-conflict lower bounds. It does not repair raw \(\beta\), \(\gamma\), \(\Delta_T\), or \(\Gamma_T\) necessity.
./iterations/iter_006/idea_02/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:69:- Maurice Sion, “On general minimax theorems,” *Pacific Journal of Mathematics* 8(1):171-176, 1958. https://projecteuclid.org/journals/pacific-journal-of-mathematics/volume-8/issue-1/On-general-minimax-theorems/pjm/1103040253.short. Relevance: background minimax theorem for finite/convex duality.
