./iterations/iter_003/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:13:   For terminal \(c\), the comparator value is \(Nc\) only as a supremum over \(a<c\). A max statement is false unless one inserts \(\eta\)-comparators \(1/2-\eta\), \(1-\eta\).
./iterations/iter_003/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:28:- **Stochastic mixture warning:** Randomly sampling the \(c_i\)’s is not automatically hard under pseudo-regret \(\sup_a \mathbb E[\cdot]-\mathbb E[\cdot]\). The proof should remain deterministic/adversarial or minimax.
./iterations/iter_003/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:34:No conflict found with the current bibliography. The result is consistent with standard individual-sequence/minimax framing and delayed-feedback lower-bound methodology.
./iterations/iter_003/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:57:3. Use \(\sup\)-regret throughout, with an optional \(\eta\)-max workaround.
./iterations/iter_003/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:59:5. Add a short warning that the theorem is universal/minimax, not instance-wise.
./iterations/iter_003/solution_gate.md:5:But the critic identified serious unresolved gaps. Most importantly, the solver mixes an instance-wise counterexample, a sufficiency theorem, and a sketched universal/minimax lower bound without fixing the quantifier regime. The concatenated block lower bound is not proved; only the finite \(0^N c\) gadget is accepted. Therefore this is not yet a complete characterization or replacement theorem.
./iterations/iter_003/solution_gate.md:11:- regret should use \(\sup_a\), not \(\max_a\), unless attainment is proved;
./iterations/iter_003/solution_gate.md:13:- any necessity statement must specify process-aware vs universal/minimax vs horizon-aware learners.
./iterations/iter_004/idea_01/explorer_01.md:18:This proves a class-uniform/minimax obstruction, not instance-wise tail necessity. It does not contradict sparse vanishing spikes or horizon/process-aware oracle collapse.
